 "standard_text": "Understand and use the laws of indices for all rational exponents; use and manipulate surds, including rationalising the denominator.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 12 A level maths worksheets on indices and surds: fractional and negative indices, index equations, simplifying surds and rationalising denominators. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "a^(m/n) is the nth root of a, raised to the power m.",
 "key_sub": "Rationalise by multiplying by the surd (or the conjugate).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>For the same base: multiply → add indices, divide → subtract, power of a power → multiply. <i>a</i><sup>0</sup> = 1, <i>a</i><sup>−n</sup> = 1/<i>a</i><sup>n</sup>, <i>a</i><sup>m/n</sup> = (ⁿ√<i>a</i>)<sup>m</sup>.</p><p>√(<i>ab</i>) = √<i>a</i>√<i>b</i>. To rationalise 1/(<i>a</i> + √<i>b</i>), multiply top and bottom by <i>a</i> − √<i>b</i>.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify √72.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>72 = 36 × 2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6√2</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 8<sup>2/3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>∛8 = 2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2² = 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying indices when multiplying powers.</li><li>Thinking a negative index gives a negative number.</li><li>Writing √a + √b = √(a + b).</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does a fractional index mean?",
   "a": "a^(1/n) is the nth root of a, and a^(m/n) is that root raised to the power m."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you rationalise a denominator?",
   "a": "Multiply the top and bottom by the surd, or by the conjugate a − √b when the denominator is a + √b."
  }
 ],
